---
Part X, Chapter 15: The client
Package: site/src/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The client

The client is the website's update and its boot. Most messages change one field and run nothing. The example does more, advancing a frame at a time while it plays. Every other animation on the site is an SVG that drives itself.

## The update return

The update returns the next model and the commands to run, as a record of the two that Foldkit calls `Return`. A step that runs nothing leaves the commands out.

=>

import { Array, Effect, Filter, Match, Option, pipe, Schema as S, Stream } from 'effect'
import { Dom, Runtime, Subscription } from 'foldkit'
import { define } from 'foldkit/command'
import { afterCommit } from 'foldkit/render'
import { animationFrameEntry } from 'foldkit/subscription'
import type { Return } from 'foldkit/update'
import { view } from './view'
import {
  ClosedSearch,
  Copied,
  CopyReset,
  drawsInPlace,
  FocusedPalette,
  Found,
  SearchGroup,
  GrewCard,
  type Message,
  Model,
  type Player,
  SectionScrolled,
  SelectedSection,
  SpottedSection,
  StageMoved,
  Ticked,
  ToggledFullHeight,
  MovedCaret,
  OpenedSearch,
  pathOf,
} from './model'
import { CHARACTERS, RUN, struckBy } from './example'

type Step = Return<Model, Message>

## The timed commands

One command carries a timer. Copying writes the text to the clipboard, waits out the flash, and resets — the write may fail on a browser that refuses the clipboard, so it flashes regardless of whether it landed, since a button that silently does nothing is worse than one that lies.

=>

const CopyThenReset = define('CopyThenReset', {
  args: { text: S.String },
  messages: [CopyReset],
  execute: ({ text }) =>
    Effect.tryPromise(() => navigator.clipboard.writeText(text)).pipe(
      Effect.ignore,
      Effect.andThen(Effect.sleep('1400 millis')),
      Effect.as(CopyReset()),
    ),
})


## Focus where the reader will type

A palette that opens without the keyboard's focus makes a reader click into it before they can type, which defeats the point of opening it from the keyboard. So opening the palette runs `FocusPalette`. It waits for the render that draws the palette to commit, since the field does not exist until then, and focuses it. A field that has already gone, because the reader closed the palette in the same breath, fails to focus, and the command lets that pass quietly.

=>

const FocusPalette = define('FocusPalette', {
  messages: [FocusedPalette],
  execute: afterCommit.pipe(
    Effect.andThen(Dom.focus('.palette-input')),
    Effect.ignore,
    Effect.as(FocusedPalette()),
  ),
})

## Jumping to a section

A section in the documentation's menu points at its heading by id, and clicking it scrolls that heading into view. The scroll waits a beat first, so the browser measures where the heading sits after the menu has redrawn rather than before. `scrollIntoView` scrolls the window itself, and the heading clears the nav pinned above it by its own `scroll-margin-top` from the stylesheet. A heading that never rendered, in a chapter the reader has not opened, does nothing.

Opening a chapter scrolls the other way, back to the top of the window. A reader who opens the next chapter from the pager at the foot of the page should land on that chapter's head, not halfway down it.

=>

const scrollIntoPreview = (id: string): void =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(document.getElementById(id)), {
    onNone: () => undefined,
    onSome: (target) => target.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' }),
  })

const ScrollToSection = define('ScrollToSection', {
  args: { id: S.String },
  messages: [SectionScrolled],
  execute: ({ id }) =>
    Effect.sleep('30 millis').pipe(
      Effect.andThen(Effect.sync(() => scrollIntoPreview(id))),
      Effect.as(SectionScrolled()),
    ),
})

const ScrollToTop = define('ScrollToTop', {
  messages: [SectionScrolled],
  execute: Effect.sync(() => window.scrollTo(0, 0)).pipe(Effect.as(SectionScrolled())),
})

## Finding your place in the menu

The menu lists every chapter of the book, so it runs taller than the window and scrolls inside its own card. Each chapter is a page of its own, and the browser opens every page with the menu scrolled to its top. Left at that, a reader who opens a chapter far down the list lands with that chapter out of sight, along with the sections listed beneath it.

So once the page is up, the client scrolls each menu until the open chapter is its first line. Two menus can hold the open chapter: the card beside the article, and the drawer that stands in for the card on a narrow screen.

`chapterOffset` measures how far the open chapter's row sits below the menu's first line, the label of the first part. Scrolling the menu to exactly that distance lifts the chapter's row into the label's place, with the chapter's sections listed under it. Every chapter follows the same rule, so the menu opens the same way on every page. A chapter near the end of the book cannot rise that far, and there the menu stops at its foot.

The book's first chapter is the one exception. Its row is already the first under its part's label, so the menu leaves it there, at offset nought, with the label in view. Opening the first chapter from the menu then looks the same as opening the source page from the nav.

=>

const MENUS = '.menu-card, .drawer-body'

const chapterOffset = (menu: HTMLElement): Option.Option<number> =>
  Option.all([
    Option.fromNullishOr(menu.firstElementChild),
    Option.fromNullishOr(menu.querySelector('.menu-item')),
    Option.fromNullishOr(menu.querySelector('.menu-item.here')),
  ]).pipe(
    Option.map(([first, opening, chapter]) =>
      chapter === opening
        ? 0
        : chapter.getBoundingClientRect().top - first.getBoundingClientRect().top,
    ),
  )

const revealChapter = (menu: HTMLElement): void =>
  Option.match(chapterOffset(menu), {
    onNone: () => undefined,
    onSome: (offset) => menu.scrollTo({ top: offset }),
  })

~

The stylesheet hides one of the two menus at every width: the drawer on a wide screen, the card on a narrow one. A hidden menu measures zero, so it stays at its top. A page with no menu has no open chapter to find, and the command does nothing there.

`RevealChapter` scrolls the menus twice. The first time is as soon as the hydrated page has painted, so the reader never watches the menu sit at its top and then jump. The page may still be drawing in a fallback font at that moment. When the web fonts arrive they can rewrap a long chapter title and move every row below it, so the chapter's row lands a little off. So the command waits for the page to finish loading, with its fonts, and scrolls the menus once more. It answers with the same quiet `SectionScrolled` the other scrolls answer with.

=>

const revealChapters = Effect.sync(() =>
  Array.fromIterable(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(MENUS)),
).pipe(
  Effect.flatMap((menus) =>
    Effect.forEach(menus, (menu) => Effect.sync(() => revealChapter(menu)), { discard: true }),
  ),
)

const pageLoaded = Effect.promise(
  () =>
    new Promise<void>((resolve) =>
      document.readyState === 'complete'
        ? resolve()
        : window.addEventListener('load', () => resolve(), { once: true }),
    ),
)

const RevealChapter = define('RevealChapter', {
  messages: [SectionScrolled],
  execute: afterCommit.pipe(
    Effect.andThen(revealChapters),
    Effect.andThen(pageLoaded),
    Effect.andThen(Effect.promise(() => document.fonts.ready)),
    Effect.andThen(revealChapters),
    Effect.as(SectionScrolled()),
  ),
})

## Leaving for the source page

The search palette moves between pages without a link, by the message alone, so it can ask for the source page from a page that holds no book. Switching there on the spot would draw the source page's frame around nothing. So the update keeps the current page and runs `LoadPage`, which hands the address to the browser. The browser fetches the source page, already drawn by the server, and keeps the current page up until it arrives. `LoadPage` answers with `ClosedSearch`, so the palette is shut by the time the page goes.

=>

const LoadPage = define('LoadPage', {
  args: { href: S.String },
  messages: [ClosedSearch],
  execute: ({ href }) =>
    Effect.sync(() => window.location.assign(href)).pipe(Effect.as(ClosedSearch())),
})

## Landing on the words a reader searched for

A result in the palette names a section and the words that matched in it. Opening a section is not enough on its own. The browser scrolls to the section's heading, and a reader who searched for one phrase in a long section is left to hunt for it. So the page a result opens marks the words that matched and scrolls them to the middle of the window.

The words often have to cross a page load. Most results lead to another page, which the browser fetches and draws afresh, and nothing in the client survives the trip. So before it leaves, the client writes a landing into the tab's session storage: the address it is going to, the words to pick out there, and the few characters before them that tell this occurrence from any other. The page that opens reads the landing back once it has booted, removes it, and lands on the words only if the landing names that very page. A landing left behind by some other trip is dropped unread.

=>

const LANDING = 'loom-landing'

const Landing = S.Struct({ address: S.String, words: S.String, before: S.String, area: S.Boolean })
type Landing = typeof Landing.Type

const addressOf = (url: URL): string => `${url.pathname}${url.hash}`

const keepLanding = (landing: Landing): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.try(() => sessionStorage.setItem(LANDING, JSON.stringify(landing))).pipe(Effect.ignore)

const decodeLanding = S.decodeUnknownOption(Landing)

const takeLanding: Effect.Effect<Option.Option<Landing>> = Effect.try(() => {
  const kept = sessionStorage.getItem(LANDING)
  sessionStorage.removeItem(LANDING)
  return kept
}).pipe(
  Effect.map((kept) =>
    pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(kept),
      Option.flatMap(Option.liftThrowable((json: string): unknown => JSON.parse(json))),
      Option.flatMap(decodeLanding),
    ),
  ),
  Effect.catchCause(() => Effect.succeed(Option.none())),
)

~

Landing reads only the page's own content, inside its `main` element, and skips the text a reader does not read there: the nav and the chapter menu, the footer, the script that carries the page's model, and anything the page is not showing, such as the drawer that stands in for the menu on a narrow screen. The menu often holds the very words a reader searched for, and a match there would mark the wrong place.

An address names a section in one of two ways. In the docs and the book it names the section's heading, and the section's text follows the heading rather than sitting inside it. There landing reads the content after the heading. A section's heading often holds the same words as its text, so the text comes first, and the heading answers only when the words are nowhere after it. That is the case when a section matched by its heading alone.

On the site's own pages the address names the section itself, an element that holds its heading and its text together. There landing reads inside the section first, and only then the content after it. Reading only after it would pass over the whole section and land on the next page's worth of words, such as the pager at the foot of the page.

An address with no section reads the whole content from the top.

A line found by meaning is the palette's window onto a section, and the window is cut from the search index's text, which joins the section's paragraphs and items with spaces. On the page those are separate blocks with nothing between them, so a line that runs from the end of one block into the next is nowhere on the page as a whole. When a line is not found, landing tries again with its first half, then the half of that, down to twelve characters. A shorter line is less sure of its place, but it still lands in the right section, and the block it lands in is the one the line began in.

=>

const textsIn = (node: Node): ReadonlyArray<Text> =>
  node instanceof Text ? [node] : Array.flatMap(Array.fromIterable(node.childNodes), textsIn)

const readable = (text: Text): boolean =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(text.parentElement), {
    onNone: () => false,
    onSome: (parent) => !parent.closest('script, style, nav, footer') && parent.checkVisibility(),
  })

const contentOf = (): Node =>
  Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(document.querySelector('main')), (): Node => document.body)

const headingOf = (url: URL): Option.Option<HTMLElement> =>
  Option.fromNullishOr(document.getElementById(decodeURIComponent(url.hash.slice(1))))

const follows = (heading: Node, text: Text): boolean =>
  !heading.contains(text) && (heading.compareDocumentPosition(text) & Node.DOCUMENT_POSITION_FOLLOWING) !== 0

const isHeading = (element: HTMLElement): boolean => /^H[1-6]$/.test(element.tagName)

~

Within those texts the landing looks first for the lead-in and the words together, the occurrence the reader was shown, and then for the words alone. A match can cross from one run of text into the next, where a word in a sentence meets an inline code span, so the texts are searched as one string. Each run remembers where it starts in that string, and a match maps back to a start in one run and an end in another. The string folds every whitespace character to a plain space, one for one. A line break in the page's markup then matches the space the search index holds in its place, and every offset still points at the same character.

=>

type Run = { readonly text: Text; readonly start: number }

const runsOf = (texts: ReadonlyArray<Text>): ReadonlyArray<Run> =>
  Array.zipWith(texts, Array.scan(texts, 0, (at, text) => at + text.data.length), (text, start) => ({
    text,
    start,
  }))

const folded = (text: string): string => text.toLowerCase().replace(/\s/g, ' ')

const runAt = (runs: ReadonlyArray<Run>, offset: number, ending: boolean): Option.Option<Run> =>
  Array.findFirst(runs, (run) =>
    ending
      ? offset > run.start && offset <= run.start + run.text.data.length
      : offset >= run.start && offset < run.start + run.text.data.length,
  )

const offsetIn = (flat: string, sought: string): Option.Option<number> =>
  Option.liftPredicate(flat.indexOf(folded(sought)), (at) => sought !== '' && at >= 0)

const rangeIn = (texts: ReadonlyArray<Text>, words: string, before: string): Option.Option<Range> => {
  const runs = runsOf(texts)
  const flat = folded(Array.join(Array.map(texts, (text) => text.data), ''))
  return pipe(
    offsetIn(flat, `${before}${words}`),
    Option.map((at) => at + before.length),
    Option.orElse(() => offsetIn(flat, words)),
    Option.flatMap((start) =>
      pipe(
        Option.all([runAt(runs, start, false), runAt(runs, start + words.length, true)]),
        Option.map(([first, last]) => {
          const range = document.createRange()
          range.setStart(first.text, start - first.start)
          range.setEnd(last.text, start + words.length - last.start)
          return range
        }),
      ),
    ),
  )
}

const shorterLine = (landing: Landing): Option.Option<Landing> =>
  Option.liftPredicate(
    { ...landing, words: landing.words.slice(0, Math.floor(landing.words.length / 2)).trim() },
    (shorter) => landing.area && shorter.words.length >= 12,
  )

const rangeOf = (url: URL, landing: Landing): Option.Option<Range> =>
  pipe(
    rangeAt(url, landing),
    Option.orElse(() => Option.flatMap(shorterLine(landing), (shorter) => rangeOf(url, shorter))),
  )

const rangeAt = (url: URL, landing: Landing): Option.Option<Range> => {
  const texts = Array.filter(textsIn(contentOf()), readable)
  return Option.match(headingOf(url), {
    onNone: () => rangeIn(texts, landing.words, landing.before),
    onSome: (target) => {
      const inside = Array.filter(texts, (text) => target.contains(text))
      const after = Array.filter(texts, (text) => follows(target, text))
      return isHeading(target)
        ? pipe(
            rangeIn(after, landing.words, landing.before),
            Option.orElse(() => rangeIn(textsIn(target), landing.words, '')),
          )
        : pipe(
            rangeIn(inside, landing.words, landing.before),
            Option.orElse(() => rangeIn(after, landing.words, landing.before)),
          )
    },
  })
}

~

Once found, the words take a mark and scroll smoothly to the middle of the window. The page has usually just jumped to the section's heading, so the reader watches the page travel on from the heading to the words and sees exactly where they landed.

The mark is a highlight rather than a selection. A selection wears the site's quiet green wash, made for text the reader picked out, and inside a coloured command chip that wash all but vanishes. So the words are registered as a highlight named `found`, which the stylesheet paints as an amber marker, the colour a browser's own find paints its matches. A highlight leaves the page's markup alone, so the view Foldkit owns is never touched. It stays until the reader's next press anywhere on the page, which clears it. A browser without highlights selects the words instead.

A result found by meaning marks an area instead of words. The section was found because the whole of it lay near the question, so no word in it deserves the marker. Its landing finds the line the palette showed and marks the block that line sits in: its paragraph, its list item, its code block, or its heading. The area takes a highlight of its own, `found-area`, which the stylesheet washes in the same amber at a third of its strength, since a whole paragraph in full marker would shout. An area taller than most of the window scrolls to its top rather than its middle, so the reader starts reading it from its first line.

=>

const FOUND = 'found'

const FOUND_AREA = 'found-area'

const BLOCKS = 'p, li, pre, blockquote, td, th, dd, dt, figcaption, h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6'

const areaOf = (range: Range): Range =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(range.startContainer.parentElement?.closest(BLOCKS)),
    Option.match({
      onNone: () => range,
      onSome: (block) => {
        const area = document.createRange()
        area.selectNodeContents(block)
        return area
      },
    }),
  )

const highlightWords = (range: Range, name: string): Option.Option<void> =>
  pipe(
    Option.liftPredicate(globalThis.CSS, (css) => css !== undefined && 'highlights' in css),
    Option.map((css) => {
      css.highlights.set(name, new Highlight(range))
      window.addEventListener('pointerdown', () => css.highlights.delete(name), { once: true })
    }),
  )

const selectWords = (range: Range): void =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(window.getSelection()), {
    onNone: () => undefined,
    onSome: (selection) => {
      selection.removeAllRanges()
      selection.addRange(range)
    },
  })

const scrollTo = (range: Range): void =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(range.startContainer.parentElement), {
    onNone: () => undefined,
    onSome: (element) =>
      element.scrollIntoView({
        behavior: 'smooth',
        block: range.getBoundingClientRect().height > window.innerHeight * 0.6 ? 'start' : 'center',
      }),
  })

const markWords = (landing: Landing) => (found: Range): void => {
  const marked = landing.area ? areaOf(found) : found
  Option.match(highlightWords(marked, landing.area ? FOUND_AREA : FOUND), {
    onNone: () => selectWords(marked),
    onSome: () => undefined,
  })
  scrollTo(marked)
}

const here = (): string => addressOf(new URL(window.location.href))

const land: Effect.Effect<void> = takeLanding.pipe(
  Effect.map((landing) =>
    pipe(
      landing,
      Option.filter((kept) => kept.address === here()),
      Option.flatMap((kept) =>
        Option.map(rangeOf(new URL(kept.address, window.location.href), kept), (found) => markWords(kept)(found)),
      ),
    ),
  ),
)

~

`OpenFound` opens a result. It keeps the landing and hands the address to the browser, as `LoadPage` does. A result on another page lands when that page boots. A result on the page already showing never reloads it: the browser only moves to the section, so `OpenFound` lands on the words itself a moment later, once the palette has closed.

`LandOnFound` is the half that runs on boot. It waits, as `RevealChapter` does, for the page to finish loading and its fonts to arrive, because a font that arrives late can rewrap the text and move the words after they were scrolled into view.

=>

const onThisPage = (url: URL): boolean => url.pathname === window.location.pathname

const OpenFound = define('OpenFound', {
  args: { href: S.String, words: S.String, before: S.String, area: S.Boolean },
  messages: [ClosedSearch],
  execute: ({ href, words, before, area }) => {
    const url = new URL(href, window.location.href)
    return keepLanding({ address: addressOf(url), words, before, area }).pipe(
      Effect.andThen(Effect.sync(() => window.location.assign(href))),
      Effect.andThen(
        onThisPage(url) ? Effect.sleep('30 millis').pipe(Effect.andThen(land)) : Effect.void,
      ),
      Effect.as(ClosedSearch()),
    )
  },
})

const LandOnFound = define('LandOnFound', {
  messages: [SectionScrolled],
  execute: afterCommit.pipe(
    Effect.andThen(pageLoaded),
    Effect.andThen(Effect.promise(() => document.fonts.ready)),
    Effect.andThen(land),
    Effect.as(SectionScrolled()),
  ),
})

## Asking the server

The palette's search runs on the server, which holds the index of every page. `Search` asks it for a query's results. It waits a moment first, so a reader typing a word sends one request for the word rather than one for every letter. A request for each letter would still go out, but each arrives after the next letter has started a newer one, and the update keeps only the answer to the query still in the field. So `Search` answers with `Found`, naming the query it answered and whether it searched by exact words. A search asks for exact words with the `exact` parameter, and otherwise lets the server search by meaning. A server that cannot be reached, or an answer that does not decode, finds nothing rather than failing the palette. A query of fewer than two characters is never sent, since the server finds nothing for it either.

=>

const SETTLE = '140 millis'

const searchable = (query: string): boolean => query.trim().length >= 2

const decodeGroups = S.decodeUnknownOption(S.Array(SearchGroup))

const searchAddress = (query: string, exact: boolean): string =>
  `/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}${exact ? '&exact=1' : ''}`

const Search = define('Search', {
  args: { query: S.String, exact: S.Boolean },
  messages: [Found],
  execute: ({ query, exact }) =>
    Effect.sleep(SETTLE).pipe(
      Effect.andThen(
        Effect.tryPromise(() => fetch(searchAddress(query, exact)).then((response) => response.json())),
      ),
      Effect.map((body) => Option.getOrElse(decodeGroups(body), () => [])),
      Effect.catchCause(() => Effect.succeed([])),
      Effect.map((groups) => Found({ query, exact, groups })),
    ),
})

## Moving the stage under the reader

The transport moves the stage to three places, and `StageMark` names them. The caret is where the run wants it while the document is being typed. The foot is where skipping and the end of a run leave it. The head is where the back button puts it, since a reader asking for the document from the start is asking to see its first line, not the line they happened to stop on.

An editor does not creep a line at a time. It holds still while the caret runs down the visible text, then steps when the caret would fall off the bottom. So the caret's mark scrolls three lines on rather than one, and only once the caret is within three lines of the foot. A line is twenty-eight pixels, which is the stage's own size at its own leading.

Every mark is clamped to the room the stage actually has, which is what answers a stage with nothing to scroll: the room is zero or less, and every mark comes out at the top.

=>

const LINE = 28
const TAIL = LINE * 3

const StageMark = S.Literals(['caret', 'foot', 'head'])
type StageMark = typeof StageMark.Type

const markedTop = (mark: StageMark, stage: HTMLElement): number =>
  Match.value(mark).pipe(
    Match.when('head', () => 0),
    Match.when('foot', () => stage.scrollHeight - stage.clientHeight),
    Match.when('caret', () => stage.scrollTop + TAIL),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const moveStage = (mark: StageMark): void =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(document.getElementById('loom-stage')), {
    onNone: () => undefined,
    onSome: (stage) => {
      const room = stage.scrollHeight - stage.clientHeight
      stage.scrollTop = Math.max(0, Math.min(room, markedTop(mark, stage)))
    },
  })

~

The foot waits a beat before it measures. It arrives with the whole rest of the document — and, at the end of a run, the two command buttons under it — so the stage it has to reach is taller than the one the browser has drawn. The other two marks measure at once, because neither depends on how tall the document has become.

=>

const MoveStage = define('MoveStage', {
  args: { to: StageMark },
  messages: [StageMoved],
  execute: ({ to }) =>
    (to === 'foot' ? Effect.sleep('30 millis') : Effect.void).pipe(
      Effect.andThen(Effect.sync(() => moveStage(to))),
      Effect.as(StageMoved()),
    ),
})

## Gliding to the card before it opens

Growing the card is worth nothing if the reader cannot see the whole of it, so the page comes to the card before the card takes the height. The two do not happen together: the sheet's top comes to rest first, and only then does the height open under it. Running them at once reads as the page and the card both lurching, and the reader loses which of the two moved.

The card keeps its place in the page and opens downwards, so its top does not move while the page glides. `CARD_AIR` is the air left above it when the page arrives. A page already standing within four pixels of that skips the glide, because a scroll that short reads as a twitch.

=>

const CARD_AIR = 24
const GLIDE = 320
const NUDGE = 4

const cardRest = (): Option.Option<number> =>
  Option.map(
    Option.fromNullishOr(document.getElementById('loom-card')),
    (card) => card.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.scrollY - CARD_AIR,
  )

~

The page is tweened by hand rather than left to `scrollTo`'s own smooth behaviour, because the glide has to say when it has finished and the browser's does not. Each frame reads the real clock, so a late frame lands where the elapsed time says it should rather than where the frame count would have put it, and the glide always takes its three hundred and twenty milliseconds.

The easing runs slow, fast, slow — a cubic either side of the halfway point. A page that starts and stops abruptly reads as a jump; this reads as the page being carried.

=>

const eased = (part: number): number =>
  part < 0.5 ? 4 * part * part * part : 1 - Math.pow(-2 * part + 2, 3) / 2

const glide = (from: number, distance: number, started: number): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.sync(() => Math.min(1, (performance.now() - started) / GLIDE)).pipe(
    Effect.tap((part) =>
      Effect.sync(() => window.scrollTo(0, Math.round(from + distance * eased(part)))),
    ),
    Effect.flatMap((part) =>
      part < 1
        ? Effect.sleep('16 millis').pipe(Effect.andThen(glide(from, distance, started)))
        : Effect.void,
    ),
  )

const GlideToCard = define('GlideToCard', {
  messages: [GrewCard],
  execute: Effect.suspend(() =>
    Option.match(cardRest(), {
      onNone: () => Effect.void,
      onSome: (rest) => {
        const from = window.scrollY
        return Math.abs(rest - from) <= NUDGE
          ? Effect.void
          : glide(from, rest - from, performance.now())
      },
    }),
  ).pipe(Effect.as(GrewCard())),
})

## The update

Most messages set one field: the query, the focus clamped to the results, the loom tab's view. Following a link records the page it led to and closes the palette behind it, unless the page is the source page and the client holds no book to draw it with. Then the current page stays, and `LoadPage` sends the browser for the source page instead. Opening a documentation chapter records its slug, clears the section the menu was following, since the previous chapter's headings are no longer on the page, shuts the drawer behind the reader, and scrolls back to the top. Opening the palette puts the focus on its first result and the keyboard in its field; closing it clears the query, the focus, the caret and the results together, so the palette opens empty the next time. Moving the caret records where it now sits. Typing records the query and, once it is long enough to search, marks a search as on its way and runs `Search`. Turning the exact switch flips how the palette searches, runs the search again for the query in the field, and puts the keyboard back in the field, since the reader's click took it away. Results arrive as `Found`, and the update keeps them only when they answer the query still in the field, searched the way the switch still says. The focus then rests on the first result rather than on the heading of the page it sits under, since the first result is the best one, and Enter should open it. Opening a result hands its address and its words to `OpenFound`, which leaves for the result and picks the words out once it gets there. The example's messages move a position rather than set a flag: playing from the end starts again from the first character, a frame advances the clock and asks the stage to follow the caret, and skipping jumps both the clock and the stage to the foot. Going back to the start puts the clock, the height and the stage all back where they began — the document at its first character, the card down from full height, and the stage at the head of the document rather than wherever the reader stopped reading.

Asking for full height is the one press that changes nothing at once. It runs the glide and waits: the page arrives at the card, `GrewCard` comes back, and only then does the height open. Giving the height back is immediate, since nothing has to travel first. Asking for a command's file toggles it, so pressing the same button twice closes what it opened. Selecting a section sets the active one, shuts the drawer if the section was picked from it, and asks the window to scroll to the section's heading. A section spotted while the reader scrolls sets the active one too, but runs nothing, so the outline follows the scroll rather than fighting it. Copying flashes the pressed button and runs its reset.

=>

const clamp = (value: number, max: number): number => Math.max(0, Math.min(max, value))

const firstResult = (groups: ReadonlyArray<SearchGroup>): number => (groups.length > 0 ? 1 : 0)

const CHARS_PER_SECOND = 26

const toggled = (player: Player): Player => {
  if (player.playing) {
    return { ...player, playing: false }
  }
  const from = player.struck >= CHARACTERS ? 0 : player.struck
  return {
    ...player,
    struck: from,
    beat: from === 0 ? 0 : player.beat,
    playing: true,
    output: Option.none(),
  }
}

const stepped = (model: Model, delta: number): Step => {
  const beat = model.player.beat + (Math.min(delta, 32) / 1000) * CHARS_PER_SECOND
  if (beat >= RUN) {
    return {
      model: {
        ...model,
        player: { ...model.player, struck: CHARACTERS, beat: RUN, playing: false },
      },
      commands: [MoveStage({ to: 'foot' })],
    }
  }
  const struck = struckBy(beat)
  return {
    model: { ...model, player: { ...model.player, struck, beat } },
    commands: struck === model.player.struck ? [] : [MoveStage({ to: 'caret' })],
  }
}

const update = (model: Model, message: Message): Step =>
  Match.value(message).pipe(
    Match.withReturnType<Step>(),
    Match.tagsExhaustive({
      ToggledPlay: () => ({ model: { ...model, player: toggled(model.player) } }),
      Ticked: ({ delta }) => stepped(model, delta),
      SkippedToEnd: () => ({
        model: {
          ...model,
          player: { ...model.player, struck: CHARACTERS, beat: RUN, playing: false },
        },
        commands: [MoveStage({ to: 'foot' })],
      }),
      ReplayedDocument: () => ({
        model: {
          ...model,
          player: { struck: 0, beat: 0, playing: false, output: Option.none(), full: false },
        },
        commands: [MoveStage({ to: 'head' })],
      }),
      ToggledFullHeight: () =>
        model.player.full
          ? { model: { ...model, player: { ...model.player, full: false } } }
          : { model, commands: [GlideToCard()] },
      GrewCard: () => ({ model: { ...model, player: { ...model.player, full: true } } }),
      ShowedOutput: ({ which }) => ({
        model: {
          ...model,
          player: {
            ...model.player,
            output: Option.contains(model.player.output, which)
              ? Option.none()
              : Option.some(which),
          },
        },
      }),
      StageMoved: () => ({ model }),
      SelectedSection: ({ id }) => ({
        model: { ...model, activeSection: id, drawerOpen: false },
        commands: [ScrollToSection({ id })],
      }),
      SectionScrolled: () => ({ model }),
      SpottedSection: ({ id }) =>
        id === model.activeSection ? { model } : { model: { ...model, activeSection: id } },
      WentTo: ({ route }) =>
        drawsInPlace(route) || Option.isSome(model.book)
          ? { model: { ...model, route, searchOpen: false } }
          : { model, commands: [LoadPage({ href: pathOf(route) })] },
      OpenedChapter: ({ slug }) => ({
        model: { ...model, chapter: slug, activeSection: '', drawerOpen: false },
        commands: [ScrollToTop()],
      }),
      PickedRuntime: ({ runtime }) => ({ model: { ...model, runtime } }),
      OpenedDrawer: () => ({ model: { ...model, drawerOpen: true } }),
      ClosedDrawer: () => ({ model: { ...model, drawerOpen: false } }),
      OpenedSearch: () => ({
        model: { ...model, searchOpen: true, focus: 0 },
        commands: [FocusPalette()],
      }),
      FocusedPalette: () => ({ model }),
      ClosedSearch: () => ({
        model: {
          ...model,
          searchOpen: false,
          query: '',
          focus: 0,
          caret: 0,
          searching: false,
          found: [],
        },
      }),
      MovedCaret: ({ at }) => ({ model: { ...model, caret: at } }),
      Typed: ({ query }) =>
        searchable(query)
          ? {
              model: { ...model, query, focus: 0, searching: true },
              commands: [Search({ query, exact: model.exact })],
            }
          : { model: { ...model, query, focus: 0, searching: false, found: [] } },
      ToggledExact: () => {
        const exact = !model.exact
        return searchable(model.query)
          ? {
              model: { ...model, exact, focus: 0, searching: true },
              commands: [Search({ query: model.query, exact }), FocusPalette()],
            }
          : { model: { ...model, exact }, commands: [FocusPalette()] }
      },
      Found: ({ query, exact, groups }) =>
        query === model.query && exact === model.exact
          ? { model: { ...model, found: groups, searching: false, focus: firstResult(groups) } }
          : { model },
      OpenedHit: ({ href, words, before, area }) => ({
        model,
        commands: [OpenFound({ href, words, before, area })],
      }),
      MovedFocus: ({ delta, count }) => ({
        model: { ...model, focus: clamp(model.focus + delta, Math.max(0, count - 1)) },
      }),
      Copied: ({ id, text }) => ({
        model: { ...model, copied: id },
        commands: [CopyThenReset({ text })],
      }),
      CopyReset: () => ({ model: { ...model, copied: '' } }),
    }),
  )

## Spotting the section on scroll

The menu should follow the reader, not only lead them. While the documentation is on show, a subscription watches the window scroll, finds the heading nearest the top of the article, and marks that section in the menu. `activeSectionId` reads the article's own second-level headings, the same ones the menu lists under the open chapter. It keeps the ones scrolled up past a small offset and takes the last. When none has reached the offset yet, it falls back to the first.

=>

const SPY_OFFSET = 100
const SPY_TARGETS = '.docs-article h2[id]'

const activeSectionId = (): Option.Option<string> => {
  const targets = Array.fromIterable(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(SPY_TARGETS))
  return pipe(
    Array.last(Array.filter(targets, (el) => el.getBoundingClientRect().top <= SPY_OFFSET)),
    Option.orElse(() => Array.head(targets)),
    Option.map((el) => el.id),
    Option.filter((id) => id.length > 0),
  )
}

## Listening only when there is something to hear

Every listener here is conditional. Each one asks the model whether its work is happening at all, and listens only while it is. The section spy runs only while the documentation or the source page is on show. The other two belong to the example: the frame loop advances the clock while the document plays, and the escape key gives the page back while the card stands at full height.

The spy and the escape key both listen on the window. The `windowEvents` function turns a window event into a stream and keeps only the events that name a message, so a key other than escape passes by without a sound.

Two more belong to the search palette. While it is closed, a slash opens it, unless the reader is typing into a field of their own, where a slash is just a slash. The slash that opens the palette is kept from also landing in its field, so the opening key never becomes the first character of the query.

One more belongs to the prose of the documentation and the source page. A command written inside a sentence draws as a small copy button, but the prose is HTML that Markdown produced, so the button carries no handler of its own. While either is on show, a listener on the window catches any click inside an element marked `data-copy`, and copies the command it carries, reporting it under the id the button checks for.

While the palette is open, its caret follows the field's own. The palette draws a block caret of its own rather than the browser's, so it needs the field's caret position as state. A reader moves that position by typing, by the arrow keys, by clicking, or by selecting, and no single event covers all four. So the listener takes the selection changes the document reports, and the key, pointer and input events besides, and after each one reads where the field's caret now stands. It reads nothing while the field has lost focus.

=>

const windowEvents = <E extends Event>(
  type: string,
  toMessage: (event: E) => Option.Option<Message>,
  options?: { readonly passive?: boolean },
): Stream.Stream<Message> =>
  Stream.fromEventListener<E>(window, type, options).pipe(
    Stream.filterMap(Filter.fromPredicateOption(toMessage)),
  )

const typingIn = (target: EventTarget | null): boolean =>
  target instanceof HTMLElement &&
  (target.isContentEditable || ['INPUT', 'TEXTAREA', 'SELECT'].includes(target.tagName))

const slashOpens = (event: KeyboardEvent): Option.Option<Message> =>
  event.key === '/' && !event.metaKey && !event.ctrlKey && !event.altKey && !typingIn(event.target)
    ? Option.some(OpenedSearch())
    : Option.none()

const paletteCaret = (): Option.Option<number> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('.palette-input')),
    Option.filter((field) => field === document.activeElement),
    Option.flatMapNullishOr((field) => field.selectionStart),
  )

const inlineCopy = (event: MouseEvent): Option.Option<Message> =>
  pipe(
    Option.liftPredicate(event.target, (target): target is Element => target instanceof Element),
    Option.flatMapNullishOr((target) => target.closest<HTMLElement>('[data-copy]')),
    Option.flatMapNullishOr((button) => button.dataset.copy),
    Option.map((text) => Copied({ id: `inline:${text}`, text })),
  )

const caretMoves: Stream.Stream<Message> = pipe(
  [
    Stream.fromEventListener(document, 'selectionchange'),
    Stream.fromEventListener(window, 'keyup'),
    Stream.fromEventListener(window, 'mouseup'),
    Stream.fromEventListener(window, 'input'),
  ],
  Stream.mergeAll({ concurrency: 'unbounded' }),
  Stream.filterMap(
    Filter.fromPredicateOption(() => Option.map(paletteCaret(), (at) => MovedCaret({ at }))),
  ),
)

const subscriptions = Subscription.make<Model, Message>()((entry) => ({
  sectionSpy: entry(
    { spying: S.Boolean },
    {
      modelToDependencies: (model) => ({
        spying: model.route === 'docs' || model.route === 'source',
      }),
      dependenciesToStream: ({ spying }) =>
        spying
          ? windowEvents(
              'scroll',
              () => Option.map(activeSectionId(), (id) => SpottedSection({ id })),
              { passive: true },
            )
          : Stream.empty,
    },
  ),
  inlineCopies: entry(
    { reading: S.Boolean },
    {
      modelToDependencies: (model) => ({
        reading: model.route === 'docs' || model.route === 'source',
      }),
      dependenciesToStream: ({ reading }) =>
        reading ? windowEvents<MouseEvent>('click', inlineCopy) : Stream.empty,
    },
  ),
  opening: entry(
    { closed: S.Boolean },
    {
      modelToDependencies: (model) => ({ closed: !model.searchOpen }),
      dependenciesToStream: ({ closed }) =>
        closed
          ? Dom.streamFromEventFilterMapPreventDefault({
              target: window,
              type: 'keydown',
              filterMapEvent: slashOpens,
            })
          : Stream.empty,
    },
  ),
  caret: entry(
    { open: S.Boolean },
    {
      modelToDependencies: (model) => ({ open: model.searchOpen }),
      dependenciesToStream: ({ open }) => (open ? caretMoves : Stream.empty),
    },
  ),
  typing: animationFrameEntry<Model, Message>({
    isActive: (model) => model.player.playing,
    toMessage: (delta) => Ticked({ delta }),
  }),
  shrinking: entry(
    { full: S.Boolean },
    {
      modelToDependencies: (model) => ({ full: model.player.full }),
      dependenciesToStream: ({ full }) =>
        full
          ? windowEvents<KeyboardEvent>('keydown', (event) =>
              event.key === 'Escape' ? Option.some(ToggledFullHeight()) : Option.none(),
            )
          : Stream.empty,
    },
  ),
}))

## Booting

The server has already rendered the page, and it wrote the model it rendered from into the page beside it. `Runtime.hydrate` reads that model back, runs the same `init` over it, and adopts the server's markup in place rather than drawing the page a second time. The `init` function returns the model unchanged, because the server already chose the route, the version and everything else a first render needs.

The server cannot choose how far the menu scrolls, because only the browser knows how tall the window is. So `init` runs `RevealChapter`, which brings the open chapter into the menu's view. It also runs `LandOnFound`, which picks out the words a reader searched for when the palette sent them here. Importing the stylesheet here, in the browser entry, keeps it out of the server's path.

The entry also imports the reload listener, the module in `src/reload.ts` that reloads the page when development rebuilds it. A production build gives it no address to listen on, so there it does nothing.

=>

import './landing.css'
import './reload'

const application = Runtime.makeApplication({
  Model,
  Flags: Model,
  init: (seed: Model): Step => ({ model: seed, commands: [RevealChapter(), LandOnFound()] }),
  update,
  view,
  subscriptions,
  container: document.getElementById('root'),
})

~

Hydration also checks that the page and the script come from the same build. A page rendered by one build and booted by the script of another can look identical and still mean something different, so Foldkit compares a build id the server stamps on the page with the one the client carries, and refuses a page whose id differs. The site agrees on that id without a build step of its own: the id is the entry script's path. Vite names the entry after a hash of its contents, so two builds never share one. The server reads the path from the shell it serves, and the client reads it from its own `import.meta.url`.

=>

Runtime.hydrate(application, { buildId: new URL(import.meta.url).pathname })

